const body = `<h1>1.7c — Dice Roller: Testing &amp; Integration</h1>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity rpg">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">RPG Build</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~15 min</span>
    </div>

    <h3>Testing Protocol</h3>

    <p>
        <code>DiceCheck.java</code> is an acceptance test that calls every method in
        your <code>Dice.java</code> and prints <code>PASS</code> or <code>FAIL</code>
        for each check. It does not read your code — it runs it, so a
        <code>PASS</code> means the method genuinely behaves. Download
        <a href="../data/DiceCheck.java" download>DiceCheck.java</a>, put it beside
        your <code>Dice.java</code>, and run it:
    </p>
    <pre><code>javac Dice.java DiceCheck.java
java DiceCheck</code></pre>
    <p>Both files must compile before anything runs. The last two lines count the results:</p>
    <pre><code>30 passed, 0 failed
Your Dice engine meets the spec.</code></pre>

    <p>
        A passing suite is the goal, but it is not the whole job — you still need to be
        able to explain <em>why</em> each range is what it is. For each method, prove it
        works on paper too:
    </p>

    <ol>
        <li><strong>roll(sides):</strong> Call it 20 times for a d20. Record min and
            max. Did you ever get 0? Did you ever get 21?</li>
        <li><strong>roll(num, sides):</strong> Call roll(3, 6) 20 times. Verify min
            &gt;= 3, max &lt;= 18, never outside the range.</li>
        <li><strong>skillCheck(pass):</strong> Call skillCheck(15) 20 times. Every
            result should be -1, 0, 1, or 2. Now try skillCheck(21) — why does a 1
            never appear?</li>
        <li><strong>statRoll():</strong> Generate a set of 6 stats (STR, DEX, CON, INT,
            WIS, CHA). Print them labeled. Verify each is in the 3–18 range.</li>
    </ol>

    <p><strong>Write your test results:</strong></p>
    <pre><code><span class="output">roll(20) test — 20 rolls:
Min: 1  Max: 20  (range correct? ✓/✗)

roll(3, 6) test — 20 rolls:
Min: ___  Max: ___  (range 3-18? ✓/✗)

skillCheck(15) test — 20 rolls:
Codes seen: ___  (all within -1 to 2? ✓/✗)

statRoll() — 6 stats:
STR: 12  DEX: 8  CON: 15  INT: 11  WIS: 14  CHA: 9
All in range 3-18? ✓/✗</span></code></pre>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic Checkpoint</h3>
        <p>
            A classmate's <code>roll(20)</code> isn't behaving. Where is the
            bug? Walk through their formula step by step:
        </p>
        <pre><code>return (int) Math.random() * sides;</code></pre>
        <p>
            What does it actually return, and why? Can you explain the fix to
            them in plain English?
        </p>
    </div>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Systematically test every method you write — min, max, edge cases</li>
            <li>Explain why the parentheses bug in the random formula causes 0</li>
            <li>Explain why a natural 1 beats a low difficulty and a natural 20 beats
                an impossible one</li>
            <li>Generate a full set of RPG stats (3d6 for each of 6 attributes)</li>
            <li>Use a Dice.java utility class from another file (DiceTester.java)</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-01/dice-roller.html">Student RPG Guide: Dice Roller</a></li>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-01/math-random.html">Math.random()</a> — parentheses trap</li>
    </ul>
</div>`;
